## What the law says

On **1 October 2025**, the relevant document is **_Residential Tenancies Act 1997_, Authorised Version No. 110, incorporating amendments as at 6 August 2025**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

- The property had to be advertised or offered at a **fixed rent**. The rental provider or agent must not **solicit or otherwise invite** an offer above the advertised rent. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- If the agent or provider advertised a rent and then relisted the property at a higher amount, that may be evidence of a breach—particularly if the original advertised rent was not a genuine fixed offer. The Act also prohibits **false or misleading representations about rent** in advertising. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)
- The documents do **not** establish that merely accepting an unsolicited higher offer is prohibited under the version in force on 1 October 2025. The retrieved regulations containing that additional statement are dated **25 November 2025**, so they do not cover the date in your question. [[2]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/21-3sra005-authorised.pdf)
- The Act does not specifically say that an applicant can challenge an agent’s false statement that the applicant “withdrew”. Nor do the retrieved documents establish a specific VCAT remedy for a person who applied but never became a renter. VCAT’s general application provision is framed principally for a residential rental provider or **renter under a residential rental agreement**. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf)

## What you can do and what to save

1. **Write to the agent and provider** saying you did not withdraw, asking them to correct their records and explain:
   - when and how they say you withdrew;
   - who changed the application status;
   - why the property was relisted at a higher rent; and
   - whether anyone invited or solicited offers above the advertised amount.

2. **Preserve evidence**, especially:
   - the original advertisement showing the property, date and advertised rent;
   - screenshots or saved PDFs of the later, higher-rent advertisement;
   - your completed application and confirmation of submission;
   - all emails, texts and portal messages, including the “withdrawn” email;
   - timestamps, call records and notes of conversations;
   - any message suggesting that applicants should offer more; and
   - the source of the information that another applicant offered more, clearly labelled as hearsay if you cannot verify it.

   The documents do not prescribe a special evidence list for this situation, so the above is practical evidence preservation rather than a statutory checklist.

3. **Ask Consumer Affairs Victoria whether it will investigate or record the matter.** The Act gives the Director functions including monitoring compliance and, in appropriate cases, investigating disputes referred by a renter or provider. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf) The retrieved documents do not say that CAV must investigate an unsuccessful rental applicant’s complaint.

4. If you have evidence that the higher offer was **invited or solicited**, identify that specifically: it is the clearest potential breach of the fixed-rent rule. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-08/97-109aa110-authorised.pdf) The available documents do not confirm that you can obtain the property, force acceptance of your application, or recover compensation merely because the agent marked it withdrawn.

## What the law says

As at **1 February 2026**, the relevant document is the **Residential Tenancies Act 1997, Authorised Version No. 111, dated 25 November 2025** (“the Act”). The accompanying document is the **Residential Tenancies Regulations 2021, Authorised Version No. 005, dated 25 November 2025** (“the Regulations”). [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/97-109aa111-authorised.pdf)[[2]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/21-3sra005-authorised.pdf)

- The property must be advertised or offered at a **fixed rent**. The rental provider or agent must not ask for, invite, or solicit an offer above the advertised rent. They must also not accept an unsolicited or uninvited higher offer. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/97-109aa111-authorised.pdf)
- Advertising rent in a way that is false or misleading is prohibited. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/97-109aa111-authorised.pdf)
- The prescribed rental application form says that rental auctions and rental bidding are prohibited. [[2]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/21-3sra005-authorised.pdf)
- The documents do **not** specifically say that an applicant has a right to compel an agent to process an application, reverse an alleged withdrawal, or offer the property to that applicant.
- The Act does allow the Tribunal to hear matters concerning a **proposed** residential rental agreement, and to make orders restraining a breach, requiring action, or requiring compensation. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/97-109aa111-authorised.pdf) However, the retrieved documents do not establish that you can obtain the property or compensation merely because your application was wrongly recorded as withdrawn.
- The Act’s misleading-conduct provision applies to conduct inducing a person to enter a residential rental agreement and expressly covers a false or misleading representation about rent. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/97-109aa111-authorised.pdf) On the documents alone, it is unclear whether an email falsely saying that you withdrew falls within that provision because you had not yet entered an agreement.

If the agent invited or accepted a higher rent, that may be a breach of the rental-bidding provisions. If the advertised rent was not a genuine fixed rent, the later higher listing may also support a complaint about misleading rent advertising. The fact that another applicant independently offered more is not, by itself, enough to establish that the agent solicited or accepted the higher offer.

## What you can do or ask

1. **Write to the agent immediately** and state that you did not withdraw your application. Ask them to:
   - confirm the date and time they say you withdrew;
   - identify how the withdrawal was supposedly made;
   - confirm whether your application was assessed;
   - explain why the rent was increased; and
   - confirm whether anyone was invited or allowed to offer more than the advertised rent.

2. **Ask the agent to preserve the application and all related records.** The prescribed application form says applicants should contact the rental provider or agent to check the outcome of an application. [[2]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/21-3sra005-authorised.pdf)

3. **Complain to Consumer Affairs Victoria** about possible rental bidding or misleading advertising. The Act gives the Director a function of monitoring compliance and, where appropriate, investigating disputes referred to the Director. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/97-109aa111-authorised.pdf) The retrieved documents do not state a specific complaint form, deadline, or guaranteed investigation outcome.

4. **Consider a VCAT application** if you can identify a breach connected with the proposed rental agreement. The Tribunal can make orders to restrain conduct, require action, or require compensation, and has jurisdiction over matters concerning a proposed residential rental agreement. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/97-109aa111-authorised.pdf) The documents do not confirm that VCAT would order the agent to accept your application or let the property to you.

## Evidence to save

Keep the following in its original form, with dates and times:

- the original advertisement showing the address and advertised rent;
- screenshots or PDFs showing the advertisement’s URL, date, rent and any later change;
- your completed application and every document submitted with it;
- the submission confirmation, receipt, portal status and timestamps;
- the email saying you withdrew, including its full headers if available;
- your reply stating that you did not withdraw;
- all texts, calls, portal messages and emails with the agent;
- any message or witness statement indicating that a higher offer was requested, invited or accepted; and
- a dated chronology recording what happened and when.

The prescribed application form must be used for residential rental applications, and it identifies the premises, agent, applicant, requested documents and application date; preserving those details can help establish what application was made and when. [[2]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/21-3sra005-authorised.pdf) Do not publish the application documents or other applicants’ personal information: the Act restricts use of application information to assessing suitability or complying with the Act, and requires protection against misuse and unauthorised access or disclosure. [[1]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/97-109aa111-authorised.pdf)[[2]](https://content.legislation.vic.gov.au/sites/default/files/2025-11/21-3sra005-authorised.pdf)
